    <h2>Reading Storm Damage Without Fooling Yourself</h2>

    <p>After a hard spring storm crosses Morgan County, the phone lines light up with homeowners who saw hail and assume they have a claim. Sometimes they do. Often the damage is cosmetic and not worth turning into an insurance claim at all — and knowing the difference is the single most valuable thing an honest roofer brings. Real hail damage on an asphalt shingle is a soft bruise where the granules are knocked away and the mat is exposed; it fails the roof by uncovering the asphalt to UV, but it is easy to confuse with normal granule loss from age.</p>

    <p>The practitioner move is to look at the soft metal first. Before anyone climbs up, dented gutters, bruised roof vents, and dinged flashing tell you whether hail actually hit with claim-worthy force — those are exactly what an adjuster inspects to date and size the storm. Here is what goes wrong: a homeowner files on cosmetic damage, the adjuster denies it, and the withdrawn or denied claim can still shadow the policy. A roofer who talks you out of a bad claim is worth more than one who talks you into it.</p>

    <h2>The Storm-Chaser Problem</h2>

    <p>Every Morgan County hailstorm draws out-of-state crews who work a region for a few weeks and move on. The honest limitation to say plainly: they are not all bad roofers, and some do fast, clean work. The trouble is that a roof warranty is only worth anything if the company is still reachable when a valley starts leaking two winters later. A 10-year workmanship warranty from a crew with an out-of-state plate and a magnetic door sign is a piece of paper. A local roofer with a Hartselle address, whom you can find again through the <a href="/locations/hartselle/">Hartselle contractor directory</a>, is who actually stands behind the work.</p>

    <p>The details that separate a lasting roof from a fast one are mostly invisible from the ground: ice-and-water shield laid in the valleys and around penetrations, a proper drip edge, and nails driven in the shingle's nailing zone at the right depth. Overdriven or high-nailed shingles void the manufacturer warranty and blow off in the next straight-line wind — a shortcut a hurried crew takes and a homeowner never sees until it fails.</p>

    <h2>The Subdivision Replacement Wave</h2>

    <p>Storm work is only half the market. Neighborhoods like Turtle Pond Estates and Booth Meadows were built roughly fifteen years ago, and the builder-grade architectural shingles that came on them are now at the front edge of replacement age. A 3-tab roof lasts about 18 to 22 years here; a dimensional shingle 25 to 30 — so this wave of roofs is going to need replacing on its own timeline whether a storm forces the issue or not. The tells are curling shingle edges, a gutter line full of granules that look like coarse black sand, and dark streaks that turn out to be bare fiberglass mat where the granules are gone. This same age wave is playing out in the smaller communities around town, from Danville to Eva, where the subdivision building of the 2000s put a lot of roofs on the same clock.</p>

    <h2 id="pricing-summary">What Roofing Costs in Hartselle</h2>

    <p>Installed ranges, and what moves them:</p>
    <ul class="trades-list-block">
      <li><strong>Architectural shingle replacement</strong> — about $4.50 to $6.50 per square foot; a typical 25-square single-story lands near $11,000 to $16,000.</li>
      <li><strong>Steep or multi-story roofs</strong> — priced higher for the added labor and safety staging, not the material.</li>
      <li><strong>Decking replacement</strong> — priced per sheet and unknown until the tear-off exposes it; budget a cushion for it on an older roof.</li>
      <li><strong>Repairs</strong> — a targeted leak or flashing repair runs a few hundred dollars, worth doing on a roof with real life left.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>Tear-off versus overlay is worth understanding: a second layer of shingles laid over the first is cheaper today but traps heat, hides the decking condition, and shortens the new roof's life. On all but the newest first-layer roofs, a full tear-off is the honest choice.</p>

    <h2>When to Do It</h2>

    <p>If a spring storm hit your roof, get a local inspection within a couple of weeks — most policies limit how long you have to file, and a fresh, clearly storm-dated roof is easier to claim than one you sit on for a year. If your roof is simply aging out, late summer through fall is the calm window, before the winter freeze-thaw finds the weak flashing and turns a replacement into an emergency.</p>

    <h2>Questions Hartselle Homeowners Ask</h2>

    <p><strong>Do I have storm damage worth a claim?</strong></p>
    <p>Often you can't tell from the ground. Real hail bruising knocks granules off and exposes the mat; check the gutters and vents for dents first. Not every storm makes a claim.</p>

    <p><strong>Should I use the door-knocker after a storm?</strong></p>
    <p>A warranty only matters if the company is still here in two years. Favor a local roofer with a Hartselle address over an out-of-state chaser crew.</p>

    <p><strong>How long does a roof last here?</strong></p>
    <p>3-tab about 18 to 22 years, architectural 25 to 30. Many 15-year-old subdivision roofs are entering replacement age now.</p>

    <p><strong>What does it cost?</strong></p>
    <p>Roughly $4.50 to $6.50 a square foot — about $11,000 to $16,000 for a typical single-story, more for steep roofs or bad decking.</p>

